A||N||19||mbramble||Problems with 87 4 cylinder||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||12-25-1999||11:26 PM||||I have seen similar problems posted but have yet to see one just like mine so, thought I'd give it a try. Car was bought by my daughter in 94.  Ran OK for awhile then suddenly, would barely run at all.  Put it in the shop and they replaced: coil assy, plug wires, egr valve, valve cover gasket, cleaned intake system throttle body. Just noticed the problem description says " on acceleration car stalls".  She drove it a couple of years and passed it on to her brother.  He joined the Navy a couple years ago and left it at home.  I drive it occassionally to keep the battery charged, etc. I would drive it much more often if it ran better.  The above work was done at 100K miles, it now has about 130K.<BR>So, here goes my description of the problem: It is approximately 32 ddegrees out right now. I just went out, started it right up, let it idle a couple of minutes and then took off. It ran beautifully. I could puch the accelerator and it moved out (for a 4 cyl anyway).  I stopped a couple times and started quickly and had no problem at all.  When it got to operating temp (the mark halfway between 100 and 220) I stopped and started again.  This time, it jerked and 'backfired' and almost stalled out. I slowlay accelerated and got it up to around 40-50 and punched it -- almost stopped running right then.  Lots of jerking and popping sounds from the 'carb'.  Finaly got back home!!  It idles beautifully all the time.<P>I have not checked the codes, will do that tomorrow.  But, I have checked and reset them a couple of other times (don't now remember which ones but I do see that I had written in 35 and 42 in my Repair Manual). I can't say that it always runs well 'cold', but that may be the case. I will have to try it a few more times and will probably start driving it more frequently with the goal of finally finding the problem.  I have a 16 year old but I won't let him drive it because I'm afraid he will try to accelerate to quickly, from a stop, and stall out halfway into the highway.  Very scary.<P>If anyone has any ideas where I should start, I would greatly appreciate it....<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||JetroGT||12-26-1999||12:04 AM||||Things to try. O2 sensor, or could be your TPS. I knew someone who had the same year car with the 4 banger and it had similar problems. I replaced both coil packs, O2 sensor, and the fuel filter. After that it actually had some power. When you had the coils replaced, did they replace the base that they plug into also?||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||12-26-1999||12:13 AM||||Don't know if they replaced the base or not.  All I have is the receipt with the list of things replaced.  It was my daughter's car at the time (and her problem!). But, I'd assumed if they replaced something, she would have been charged and it would appear on the bill.  It doesn't.<P>Also, meant to mention, after reading some other posts -- looking in the engine compartment in complete darkness, I don't see any stray sparks from the plug wires and there is no pink glow anywhere in the engine compartment...  Looked both when I started it up and it was running good and when I got home and it was running like crap.<P>Any chance it could have something to do with the Thermac???<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||12-26-1999||12:21 AM||||your coolant temp sensor might be bad if its due to the engine temp.  Theres a way of checking it with a multimeter, and not to expensive to replace.  Check your plug wires if it has cracks, or get a spray bottle like hair spray, and spray water on the wires to see if you get any sparks jumping.<BR>I have a 4 cylinder also, and the only time my car backfired was when the wires were badly cracked.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TooLowFiero||12-26-1999||06:00 AM||||well i had the same problem with my 86 4 banger i ended up replacing coil packs ignition control modual cap rotor plugs wires and getting the timing done it wasnt that expensive and it runs really nice now(cept for the damned idle)i have a 400 RPM variance in idle at any temp well theres my input and if anyone knows how to get rid of that stupid idle problem ide like to know how  thanks<P>TooLow||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||12-26-1999||12:26 PM||||Sun Morning update: Took it out this morning and it ran fine, until it heated up.  Then, it's barely getting around -- have to gently give it gas or it 'bogs out' and 'backfires' (these are the way it seem to me, don't know the technical terms!). Stopped in front of the house to back it in and it died 3 or 4 times before I could get in the driveway.  Running very rough.  <P>Checked the ECM for codes and there were none recorded. There doesn't seem to be a Thermac, at least not where I would expect to find it by looking at the drawing in my 'Service Manual'.  <P>There is an unconnected vacuum port in the intake manifold (I think).  If you look straight down onto the engine from the back of the car, between the transmission 'dipstick' and the TBI, there is a vacuum port.  It is not connected to anything.  I think that that this has at some time had a cap on it (a couple of different times, I think), but they seem to disappear??  Getting blown off???  What would cause that??  As I said before, this car has belonged to my kids who took care of the maintenance, I have only had dealings with the car for the past year or so, and only then on occassional drives to keep the battery charged -- just because of how it's running now.  Does anyone know what that vacuum port is for???  SHould it be capped off or connected to something???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-26-1999||12:50 PM||||Yes that port should be capped. Yes, the caps do come off, usually from old age, but sometimes they blow off durring a backfire. Check the other lines over, especially the big one for the brake booster.<P>Also go around the motor and tranny and look for ground wires. any of these loose or damaged can do odd things.<P>Don't just compair problems in the DIS ignition 4 cyl to the distributor ones... The DIS motor is completely computer controlled. The Distributor motor, while computer controlled, requires some manual adjustments not possible or needed with DIS.<P>At this millage I'd replace the TPS sensor. It likely is worn baddly. In this case I'd also replace the crank sensor which I think is part of the ingition controller under the coil set. The crank sensor is known for thermal problems. You can replace both sensors yourself with common tools. Be carefull not to strip the TBI holes replacing the TPS sensor.<P>Replace the plugs and wires as well, it's cheap insurance. DIS motors are very picky about plugs and wires. Do Not use platinum plugs. Make sure all 4 plugs are very carefully gapped to .060. Do not use altarnate number some people may tell you. It can damage the coil set.<P>Get an ECM scanner like the AutoXray GM only scanner. You can then read all the operational data, not just codes. Codes on a DIS motor can be very missleading.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||12-26-1999||06:41 PM||||Replaced the TPS and capped the open vacuum port with no change (though I think it does idle better with the vacuum port capped) -- ran good when 'cold', problems showed when it got to operating temp.  Will look at replacing the other suggested componenets in the next few days.<P>Is there anything I should do regarding driving at a certain speed, etc., after resetting the computer??  Have seen a couple references to something happening in the ECM after you drive at 35mph??  I am really lost in this computer controlled engine!!  Is there a good reference I can read that talks about how it all works, specifically the '87 4cyl??  I have the Haynes manual but I keep getting sidetracked after realizing I am looking at info for the wrong year or engine, etc....||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-26-1999||07:47 PM||||bramble,<P>the Helms manual is the factory shop book for 87. It is the only 87 specific book.<P>Get the ECM scanner I mentioned above. (Don't confuse it with the AutoXray XP240. the 240 is the universal scanner and costs about 300$us.)<P>The ECM will reset the IAC motor only after the car is driven at 35+mph for about 1 minute. IAC controls idle.<P>Was the EGR replaced on this thing? either way check and make sure it's not loose. If it is loose take it off and make sure it didn't pop the gasket or plug with carbon. If in doubt of it's condition, replace it.<P>For Others,<BR>I've been doing some testing. The ECM will see a ready O2 sensor within about 60-90 seconds on the DIS motor. I expect the same of the other motors. I was very suprised it warmed up that fast. even in cold weather. So I don't think his problem is that sensor.<P>Could be a temp sensor either coolant or intake manifold, or a couple other things. It's hard to say without scanning it. I know I harp on the scanner allot. It really is about the best 150$ I've spent on the car. In this case the scanner would report all the temp sensor values and tell you in 1 minute if they are good/bad at any given operating temp. You'd also know if the ECM is getting accurate RPM, MPH, and other feeds.||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||12-28-1999||08:17 PM||||STOP…Don’t throw anymore money at this thing. Stop guessing and do some diagnostics here. I am an ASE certified X-mechanic. This sounds like a classic ignition leakage problem, most likely old, damaged, or leaking wires. Try this, get a squirt bottle and put some slightly soapy water in it. Next, with this soapy water and the engine warmed up and running squirt the spark plug boots, wires, and module. If the engine dies replace the wire set. If the engine is still running try putting the engine under a load by putting your foot on the gas and brake at the same time. This puts the ignition system under an electrical load as the cylinders build pressure. That should invoke an electrical leak if the wires are bad causing the engine to stall, backfire, or buck.<P> The soapy water solution makes it easy for the electrical current in the wires to find a way out to ground. I’m positive that it is most likely a simple electrical leak, at the worst case scenario you may need new coil packs because the leaking wires will damage them over time causing them to leak like the wires as well. The engine backfiring most likely causes the missing vacuum cap. The positive pressure blows the cap off. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||12-28-1999||09:00 PM||||I just did as you suggested.  I saw no effect of the spraying water at all.  Tried it cold when the car was running good.  Then drove around till it started missing/backfiring etc., (when I press the gas more than just very, very, very slowly), then went back home and tried it again.  Both at just an idle and then with it in gear and revved up a bit.  No change in the engine sound, no sparks, nothing.<P>After trying all over town to see if I could rent or borrow a scanner with no luck (and the cheapest to buy locally is around $250), I have decided to take it to the Pontiac dealer to put it on their analyzer.  I called the guy who will do it and explained the problems and he is gonna look at it Tue morning.  Also called a local parts house and got the prices of every sensor, etc., to compare against anything the dealer says may need replacing.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sage||12-28-1999||10:34 PM||||Had similar problem with my sons 88 2.5  Replaced M.A.T. sensor and it cured it.  My 2cents||||reg||1
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||12-29-1999||02:12 PM||||I have followed ogre's post on the 87 DIS,  after I bought mine.  He knows this car very well,  (thanks ogre). One thing he always points out is replacement of the sensors as they will got out over time. The best price I've found is wrenchead.com their price is about 1/2 the parts house. I will soon have all new sensors on both the 87 and my 86SE.<P>Something to think about???||||reg||1
Z||000013||mbramble||01-04-2000||05:04 PM||||JUst got home from the local Pontiac dealer. It was one of the coil packs.  Actually, it had gotten to be pretty obvious as I drove it the past few days to make sure I would know when it was bad so I could check it after repairs.  It got to the point where it would barely run -- like maybe only hitting on a couple of cylinders all the time -- not just when it was cold.  Took off for the dealer at about 6:00 this morning and made it 5 miles before it wouldn't go anymore!!  Had to call a towing service.  Including the tow, it cost me right at $200 and it runs great.  <P>Thanks for the suggestions and help.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000014||88formula||01-05-2000||10:04 PM||||AHHHHHH-HA, I new it was ignition.||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||01-05-2000||10:05 PM||||AHHHHHH-HA, I new it was ignition related.||||reg||1
Z||000016||David Gamache||01-07-2000||01:30 AM||||Congrats on solving the problem. If you have future problems the X-mechanic is right. Buying a bunch of sensors can be costly and unnecessary. At your current mileage be prepared,if it already has not occured, to replace the fuel pump in the gas tank,the fuel injector, and watch for cracks in the exhaust manifold. These along with the plugs,wires, coils, etc...(basic ignition parts)provide most of the problems for the 4 cylinder cars. For the mileage and the fun hang in there.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||theogre||01-07-2000||07:52 PM||||While buying sensors is expencive, it's not a bad thing either. I don't just replace all the sensors.... I do replace certain ones if they are even a little flaky looking. The TPS is one that gets replaced if it even looks bad. TPS is the only sensor that is a moving part. The rest I can test with my ECM scanner.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Patrick||01-07-2000||09:00 PM||||<BR><B>Ogre</B>, you've often mentioned replacement of the <B>TPS</B> as a precaution when it's got a few years on it. That's probably good advice, but what is the reason that using a mutimeter on it for testing purposes wouldn't work? It would seem to me if it was mechanically worn out that this would show up in the electrical values registered as the throttle was moved from idle to wide open. Yes,no?||||reg||5
Z||000019||theogre||01-08-2000||01:53 PM||||I'm going to put this answer in it's own thread so we can find it again.||||reg||1
